course: dictionary
lesson: 2339
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word crusade has two main meanings. In history, the Crusades were wars from about 1095 to 1291, when Christian armies from Europe fought mainly to take control of Jerusalem and the Holy Land. Today, a crusade can also be a long, strong effort to change something that people believe is wrong.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is used. «From 1096 European Christians fought the Crusades to control Jerusalem» is about history, because it names a city and a time. «She began a crusade for clean water» is about a cause, because it talks about an effort to change something.
explain: Look for clue words. In «a crusade against litter», the word against shows that the crusade is opposing something. «To crusade» is a verb, and it means to work hard and publicly for a cause.
explain: Use the swap test. Put campaign in place of crusade. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Historians do not use campaign for the Crusades, though, so check the topic too.
fact: A crusade can mean a war in history, or a long, strong effort to change something people believe is wrong.
fact: «The Crusades», with a capital C and an s, usually means the wars from about 1095 to 1291.
fact: «On a crusade» and «a crusade against» are common phrases, and «the First Crusade» was the first of these wars.
fact: «Both sides in the Crusades did brave things and cruel things», so historians see the wars as violent and complicated.
fact: People of all three faiths, Christian, Jewish and Muslim, remember the Crusades in different ways.
exercise: «The Crusades were wars from about 1095 to 1291 fought mainly to take control of Jerusalem.» What were the Crusades? | *Wars fought by Christian armies from Europe over Jerusalem and the Holy Land | A quiet kind of walk | A type of old castle | Short sports games between schools
exercise: Mia says, «I will crusade against litter in our park.» What does crusade mean here? | A lazy afternoon sleeping in the sun | A picnic with friends | *A long, strong, public effort to change something | A small, quiet whisper
exercise: «Both sides in the Crusades did brave things and cruel things.» What do the words «both sides» suggest? | It was a holiday | *Good and bad things happened on each side | Only one side was right | Nobody fought at all
test: Which word fits best in the gap: «a ___ against litter»? | curt | ancient | *crusade | barely
test: «From 1096 European Christians fought the Crusades to control Jerusalem.» Which city were they fighting over? | Glasgow | Sydney | Rome | *Jerusalem
exam: In «She began a crusade for clean water», which word could replace crusade without changing the meaning much? | *campaign | nap | holiday | whisper
